WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.917
Four servers, powered off, on a bench.

00:00:02.917 --> 00:00:03.750
One command.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.750
Four and a half minutes later they are a
Slurm cluster that

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:12.917
has just run a job on every one of them.

00:00:12.917 --> 00:00:17.500
No console, no answer file, no manual step
in the middle.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.583
That is the whole claim,

00:00:19.583 --> 00:00:24.167
and everything after this is the machinery
that makes it true.

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:29.167
Between a powered-off machine and a job
there is a pipeline most

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:33.750
people never see: firmware, a DHCP offer
on an isolated segment,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.917
a TFTP fetch, an iPXE script, a kernel, an
initramfs,

00:00:37.917 --> 00:00:41.250
a root filesystem that arrives over the
network,

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:45.417
an identity that comes from somewhere
other than the image,

00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:47.917
a burn-in that gates the node,

00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:52.500
and a scheduler that has to be told the
node exists.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:57.083
This repository builds that pipeline and
times every phase of it,

00:00:57.083 --> 00:00:58.750
per node, every run.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.083
The timing is the deliverable, not a
by-product.

00:01:02.083 --> 00:01:03.750
Here is the run.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:07.500
`make demo N=2`: two nodes, from power-off
to Slurm-ready.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.500
`N` is a variable; nothing in the
repository knows a node count.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.250
`platform-up` is instant because the lab
is already up

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.333
every step here is idempotent,

00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:22.083
so a second run costs nothing and changes
nothing.

00:01:22.083 --> 00:01:26.250
Then `converge` applies the head node's
configuration: the network, dnsmasq,

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.917
NetBox, Warewulf, the controller.

00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:32.083
That is fifty seconds, and it is flat in
`N`

00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:36.667
the check that no per-node work leaked
into a per-run step.

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:39.583
At fifty seconds the nodes power on.

00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:40.833
Watch the phases.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:45.000
`node-define` creates each disk and
domain, `node-start` powers it on,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.917
then `firmware` power-on through UEFI and
PXE.

00:01:47.917 --> 00:01:52.500
`dhcp-lease` is the first lease on the
isolated segment this boot;

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.583
`ipxe` fetches the boot script;

00:01:54.583 --> 00:01:57.917
`kernel` and `initramfs` pull the two
early stages.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:00.000
`overlay` is the interesting one:

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.000
the root filesystem arrives over the
network and the node's identity is

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.167
layered on top, so no two nodes share a
filesystem.

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:12.917
`login` is pivot to the real root and
systemd.

00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:17.917
Each number is measured live from the
node's serial console, per node,

00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:22.083
because firmware and iPXE print no
timestamps of their own.

00:02:22.083 --> 00:02:24.583
Here is the result that matters.

00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:27.917
`platform-up` and `converge` stay flat as
`N` grows.

00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:30.417
`node-define` is almost linear and cheap.

00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:34.583
The contended phases are the lease and the
image fetch:

00:02:34.583 --> 00:02:37.500
one server answering every node at once.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.500
At four nodes, `overlay` grows from about
eight seconds to about twelve.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.500
That bend is the scale argument, and it is
a measured bend,

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.750
not a guess.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.667
A login prompt is not a cluster.

00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:53.750
A fresh node arrives `staged`;

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.333
it must pass its own burn-in before it can
take work,

00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:02.500
and the head node not the node judges the
result.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.500
On this run the gate is skipped in a tenth
of a

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:07.917
second:

00:03:07.917 --> 00:03:12.917
these two already passed on an earlier run
and NetBox already calls

00:03:12.917 --> 00:03:13.750
them `active`.

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:18.333
On the four-node run it takes a hundred
and eighty-nine seconds,

00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:23.333
because burn-in and the promotion reboots
are per node and cannot be

00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:24.167
parallelised away.

00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:25.000
Then Slurm.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
`slurmd` starts with `--conf-server`;
there is no `slurm.conf` on a node, ever.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.000
Both nodes register and the job array runs
one task per node,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:36.667
all `COMPLETED`, exit zero.

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:40.417
That is the finish line: the machines took
work.

00:03:40.417 --> 00:03:43.750
Ninety-five seconds of wall clock for this
run,

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:46.250
because the gate was already satisfied.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.167
Now the same code at four nodes.

00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:52.917
This table is a reprint of a stored run,

00:03:52.917 --> 00:03:57.500
so it is the real measurements, not a
second live run.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.000
`converge` is fifty seconds — flat.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.750
`nodes-up` is thirty-four and a half —
slightly up.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.917
The gate is a hundred and eighty-nine
linear in `N`,

00:04:07.917 --> 00:04:10.000
because burn-in is per node.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.500
The job array is four seconds.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.083
Wall clock: two hundred and eighty-six
seconds, four minutes and forty-six,

00:04:17.083 --> 00:04:18.333
for four nodes.

00:04:18.333 --> 00:04:20.417
One command, no manual step.

00:04:20.417 --> 00:04:24.167
Three things it does not prove, stated on
purpose.

00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:29.167
There is no InfiniBand hardware; the RDMA
numbers are Soft-RoCE over Ethernet,

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:32.917
a real verbs data path on an unreal
fabric.

00:04:32.917 --> 00:04:37.917
There is no real BMC; Redfish power and
boot order are real,

00:04:37.917 --> 00:04:40.000
vendor BIOS attributes are not.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.917
And nothing above four nodes is observed;

00:04:42.917 --> 00:04:47.083
the hundred-node claim is arithmetic over
a measured per-node cost,

00:04:47.083 --> 00:04:49.583
and it is labelled that way.

00:04:49.583 --> 00:04:53.333
The pipeline is code, every phase is a
number,

00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:55.833
and nothing knows the node count.

00:04:55.833 --> 00:04:57.500
That is the point.

